    "slug": "yemeni-government-forces-retake-key-bab-el-mandeb-strait-positions",
    "summary": "Yemen's Saudi-backed government says it has regained control of the Bab el-Mandeb Strait after Houthi forces seized it last month, though fighting continues.",
    "content": "Yemeni government forces, supported by Saudi Arabia, have taken effective control of the Bab el-Mandeb Strait, according to Yemen's Information Minister Moammar al-Eryani. In a statement to Reuters on Monday, al-Eryani said the military had advanced along the coast with heavy Saudi air support to secure key positions in the Dhubab area, located at the narrowest point of the strategic waterway. Fighting with Houthi forces in the vicinity is ongoing.\n\nThe Bab el-Mandeb Strait, which separates Yemen from the Horn of Africa, is a critical maritime corridor for global oil shipments. Its capture by the Iran-backed Houthi movement last month raised concerns in international energy markets and prompted a swift response from the Saudi-led coalition. The recent government offensive aimed to reverse those gains and restore control over the strait.\n\nThe Houthi takeover of the strait last month was part of the broader conflict in Yemen, where the Houthis have been fighting the internationally recognized government since 2014. The Saudi-led coalition intervened in 2015 to support the government. The Bab el-Mandeb has been a focal point of the conflict due to its strategic importance for both military and commercial shipping.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the developments in Yemen are closely watched because of the proximity of the Bab el-Mandeb to Somalia and the Gulf of Aden. Many Somali families rely on remittances and trade routes that pass through the region. Instability in Yemen can disrupt shipping lanes, affecting the cost and availability of goods in Somalia and beyond. Additionally, the presence of foreign forces and Iran-backed groups in Yemen raises security concerns for the entire Horn of Africa.\n\nThe recapture of Dhubab by government forces, if sustained, could ease some of the immediate worries about energy supplies and maritime security. However, continued clashes indicate that the situation remains volatile. The international community has called for de-escalation and a return to UN-led peace talks to end the Yemeni conflict.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/world/yemen-retakes-bab-el-mandeb-from-houthis/",
