    "summary": "Ethiopia's new parliament has re-elected Abiy Ahmed as Prime Minister, granting him a third term. The Prosperity Party, led by Abiy, won a large majority in the 2026 parliamentary elections.",
    "content": "Ethiopia's newly formed parliament has re-elected Abiy Ahmed as Prime Minister, according to a report by Goobjoog. The vote took place in Addis Ababa on October 5, 2026, marking the start of Abiy's third term in office. The session also formally inaugurated the new parliamentary body.\n\nAbiy first assumed the role of Prime Minister in 2018 following the resignation of his predecessor, Hailemariam Desalegn. He was subsequently re-elected in 2021 after that year's general elections. His Prosperity Party, which he founded in 2019, secured a substantial victory in the 2026 parliamentary polls, paving the way for his return to the top executive post.\n\nThe re-election comes amid ongoing political and economic challenges in Ethiopia, including internal conflicts and regional tensions. Abiy's previous terms were marked by significant reforms, such as the opening of political space and economic liberalization, but also by a devastating two-year war in the northern Tigray region that ended in 2022. His government has also faced criticism over human rights abuses and the handling of ethnic violence.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, Ethiopia's political stability is of particular interest due to the country's role in the Horn of Africa and its historical ties with Somalia. Ethiopia hosts a large Somali population in its eastern region and has been a key player in regional security, including peacekeeping efforts in Somalia. Changes in Ethiopia's leadership can influence bilateral relations, trade, and security cooperation, affecting diaspora families with cross-border connections.\n\nAbiy's third term will likely focus on continuing economic reforms and addressing internal security issues. The international community will watch closely, given Ethiopia's strategic importance and its involvement in regional affairs. The diaspora, with its deep ties to both Ethiopia and Somalia, will be attentive to how policies evolve under the new administration.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/05/deg-deg-abiy-axmed-oo-markii-saddexaad-loo-doortay-raiisul-wasaaraha-itoobiya/",
