    "content": "Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud and General Abdel Fattah al-Burhan, head of Sudan's Transitional Sovereignty Council, held a meeting in New Alamein, Egypt, on October 5, 2026. The two leaders were in Egypt to attend a consultative summit that also included the presidents of Egypt and Eritrea. The gathering focused on political and security developments in the Horn of Africa and the Red Sea region. The four leaders issued a joint call for respect for the sovereignty, unity, and territorial integrity of all states.\n\nThe meeting comes amid heightened tensions over Red Sea security, where attacks on shipping have disrupted global trade and drawn international military attention. For Sudan, General al-Burhan's transitional council has been grappling with a devastating internal conflict since 2023, and his participation in regional talks underscores efforts to maintain diplomatic engagement despite the war. Somalia, meanwhile, continues to face its own security challenges, including the fight against al-Shabaab and a dispute with Ethiopia over a port deal that has strained regional relations.\n\nEgypt has sought to position itself as a mediator and key player in Red Sea and Horn of Africa affairs, hosting this summit as part of that effort. Eritrea's inclusion signals a broader attempt to bring together often divergent regional voices. The joint statement on sovereignty and unity is particularly sensitive for Somalia, which has strongly opposed any infringement on its territorial integrity, especially regarding the Ethiopia-Somaliland memorandum of understanding.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are closely watched. Many in the diaspora have family ties to both Somalia and Sudan, and the prospect of greater regional stability could ease humanitarian concerns and improve conditions for remittances and travel. The diaspora also plays a vital role in supporting livelihoods back home, and any escalation in the Red Sea or Horn of Africa directly affects shipping routes and aid delivery.\n\nThe four-nation summit did not produce detailed public commitments beyond the stated principles. However, the fact that Somalia and Sudan's leaders met on the margins suggests continued dialogue among key regional actors. Analysts note that while such gatherings can help reduce misunderstandings, concrete outcomes will depend on follow-up actions and the willingness of all parties to uphold the declared principles.\n\nThe Somali government has not yet issued a separate readout of the bilateral meeting, and further details may emerge in the coming days. Ramadpay Daily News will continue to monitor the situation and provide updates as they become available. Read the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/05/madaxda-soomaaliya-iyo-suudaan-oo-kulan-ku-yeeshay-al-alamein/",
